          %h2#general General
          %p
            The word ‘period’ has many meanings in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periodic+function"} periodic function
            comes from the intuition that the periodicity is in the time dimension. Functions on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tori"} tori
            are periodic in two directions, say the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weierstrass+elliptic+functions"} Weierstrass elliptic functions
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curves"} elliptic curves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
          %h2#InDifferentialGeometry In differential geometry
          %p
            The
            %em period
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+differential+form"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \Omega^n_{cl}(X)
            over an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integral
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi S
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_S \omega
          %h2#InNumberTheory In number theory and algebraic geometry
          %p
            There is another deep notion of periods in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory"} number theory
            and a more specific version related to specific situations in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            divide into algebraic and transcendental.
            %strong Periods
            are more general than algebraic numbers: they are those (complex) numbers which can be obtained as integrals of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+function"} algebraic function
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+number"} algebraic number
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semialgebraic+set"} semialgebraic set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            of complex numbers bigger than the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            of algebraic numbers. There are several operations which lead to new periods. In fact, if we view them abstractly, as integrals of some abstract function over an abstract semialgebraic set, then we can take unions of such sets, do partial integration and so on. There is a conjecture that there are no relations among periods except those of a short list of such obvious relations!
          %p
            Periods appear in a number of situations in classical algebraic geometry. Specific matrices of periods are defined and important in the theory of algebraic functions,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            for algebraic cycles, the study of actions of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
          %h2#references References
          %p Overview:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claire+Voisin"}> Claire Voisin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em From Analysis situs to the theory of periods
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8RIqZX4hKqs"} yt
          %h3#in_differential_geometry_2 In differential geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+de+Rham"}> Georges de Rham
              , p. 135 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Differentiable Manifolds – Forms, Currents, Harmonic Forms
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 266
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61752-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-61752-2
          %h3#ReferencesInNumberTheory In number theory
          %p A general introduction to and discussion of algebraic periods is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Don+Zagier"} Don Zagier
              = succeed "," do
                %em Periods
              %a{:href => "http://193.51.104.7/~maxim/TEXTS/Periods.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            which in section 3 discusses the appearance of periods as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+values+of+L-functions"} special values of L-functions
          %p A popularization is in
          %ul
            %li
              Stefan Müller-Stach,
              = succeed "," do
                %em What is a period ?
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1407.2388"} arxiv/1407.2388
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+B.+Goncharov"}> A. B. Goncharov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Periods and mixed motives
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0202154"} math.AG/0202154
            %li
              %p
                mathoverflow:
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/20497/is-it-known-that-the-ring-of-periods-is-not-a-field"} ring of periods not a field
            %li
              %p
                J. Carlson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phillip+Griffith"} Phillip Griffith
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What is a period domain?
                %strong 55
                11 (2008) 1418-1419 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/200811/tx081101418p.pdf"}> pdf
                , full issue:
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/200811/200811FullIssue.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#ReferencesInPerturbativeQuantumFieldTheory In perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives+in+physics"} motives in physics
            via periods as appearing in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
            originates in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Operads and motives in deformation quantization
              %strong 48
              (1999) 35–72,
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/9904055"} math.QA/9904055
          %p More details on this (and a good review of periods in the first place) is in
          %ul
            %li
              Annette Huber, Stefan Müller-Stach,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the relation between Nori motives and Kontsevich periods
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1105.0865"} 1105.0865
          %p and briefly in section 8.5 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noncommutative+Geometry%2C+Quantum+Fields+and+Motives"} Noncommutative Geometry, Quantum Fields and Motives
          %p
            Discussion in the rigorous Lorentzian context of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Rejzner16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"}> Kasia Rejzner
              ,
              %em Renormalization and periods in perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02748"}> arXiv:1603.02748
              )
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives+in+physics"} motives in physics
